 with an Ermine.""}, {"sourceSlug": "portraiture-evolution", "sourceName": "Evolution of Portraiture", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "lady-with-an-ermine", "targetName": "Lady with an Ermine", "context": "The "Lady with an Ermine" defined new standards in portraiture, influencing future artistic representations of women."}], "places": [{"name": "Milan, Italy", "role": "Artistic center"}, {"name": "Florence, Italy", "role": "Cultural influence"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Later exhibition site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Lady with an Ermine", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "lady-with-an-ermine"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q474338", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_with_an_Ermine"], "tags": ["painting", "Paintings & Visual Art", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Lady with an Ermine contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to artistic heritage and cultural identity. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The "Lady with an Ermine" significantly influenced the portrayal of women in art, marking a shift towards more individualized representations during the Renaissance. Its impact on the evolution of portraiture is evident in the works of subsequent artists, ensuring its relevance in art history.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T01:37:24Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27317765776", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Painting by leonardo da vinci Created by Leonardo da Vinci. Created c. 1490.", "newValue": "The "Lady with an Ermine," painted by Leonardo da Vinci around 1490, captures the essence of the Italian Renaissance and reflects the cultural and artistic advancements of the era. This portrait, depicting Cecilia Gallerani, a young woman who was the mistress of Ludovico Sforza, Duke of Milan, holds significant importance in the canon of Western art due to its innovative portrayal of femininity and the intricate use of chiaroscuro. Da Vinci's mastery of oil on wood not only showcases his technical prowess but also symbolizes the shifting societal norms that began to elevate the status of women in the arts and beyond during the late 15th century.

Commissioned by Ludovico Sforza, the work is notable for its psychological depth and the lifelike representation of its subject. The ermine, a symbol of purity and moderation, serves as a metaphor for Gallerani's virtue and her connection to the Sforza court. Leonardo's technique, characterized by delicate brushwork and a focus on the human expression, transforms the painting into a narrative that engages viewers with the subject's inner world. The work was created during a time of political turmoil in Milan, as the city navigated between French and Spanish influences, which added layers of complexity to its cultural output.

